<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">United Arab Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1958 CE – 1961 CE · capital Cairo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The union of Egypt and Syria under Gamal Abdel Nasser, the high point of pan-Arab nationalism.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Syrian officers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Ba'athist</a> politicians, alarmed by communist influence and inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser's</a> prestige after the 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez crisis</a>, asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for union. On 1 February 1958 the United Arab Republic was proclaimed, with Nasser as president and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> as capital; a plebiscite confirmed it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> became the northern region of a centralised state. Political parties were dissolved, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Ba'ath</a>, and Egyptian officials and security services dominated, with Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdel-hakim-amer">Abdel Hakim Amer</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser's</a> representative in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. Land reform and the nationalisation of banks and industry in 1961 alienated Syrian landowners and merchants. A loose confederation with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yemen-arab-republic">North Yemen</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-states">United Arab States</a>, was also formed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 28 September 1961 Syrian officers seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and declared secession. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> kept the name United Arab Republic until 1971.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-arab-republic.jpg" alt="United Arab Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details.; For further informations, see also: en:Syrian Arab Republic and the en:United Arab Republic. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Arab_Republic_(1958%E2%80%931971),_Flag_of_Syria_(1980%E2%80%932024).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1958 CE – 1961 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cairo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gamal Abdel Nasser, Abdel Hakim Amer</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>UAR</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1960&amp;focus=united-arab-republic" aria-label="United Arab Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5491 1081 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where United Arab Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5811 1337L5927 1218M5811 1337L5927 1218M5811 1337L5811 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5927 1218h0M5927 1218h0M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1337h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cairo. <a href="/atlas?year=1960&amp;focus=united-arab-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
